# Additional confirmed scope from the requirements interview on 31 October 2026.
def expand_requirements(sections):
 def find(prefix):return next(s for s in sections if s['title'].startswith(prefix))
 def request(s,rid,status,text):s['blocks'].append(('req',rid,status,text))
 def paragraph(s,text):s['blocks'].append(('p',text))
 def matrix(s,headers,rows,widths):s['blocks'].append(('table',headers,rows,widths))
 def sec(title,intro):return {'title':title,'intro':intro,'blocks':[]}
 def replace_text(value):
  if isinstance(value,str):
   return value.replace('Open decisions are listed in Section 18.','Open decisions are listed in Section 22.').replace('Exact toolkit versions, schemas','Exact egui versions, schemas')
  if isinstance(value,list):return [replace_text(x) for x in value]
  if isinstance(value,tuple):return tuple(replace_text(x) for x in value)
  if isinstance(value,dict):return {k:replace_text(v) for k,v in value.items()}
  return value
 sections[:]=[replace_text(s) for s in sections]
 product=find('1 ')
 product['blocks'][0]=('p','This document consolidates the conversation and reviewed UI prototype, including egui selection and the added scope for external MCP servers, comprehensive Settings, GitHub configuration, scheduled jobs, manual CodeGraph operations, database indexing, graph and schema exploration, repository health, code reviews, conventions, skills, tool access, and indexing diagnostics. It remains a review draft; the remaining decisions are being resolved through a Socratic interview before implementation.')
 decisions=next(b for b in find('2 ')['blocks'] if b[0]=='table')[2]
 decisions[1][1]='Rust with egui is selected for the native product UI. A web UI or WebView must not be required. Exact egui version and supporting native integration crates remain implementation choices.'
 decisions.extend([
 ['DEC 15','MCP supports both directions: external servers alongside built-in tools, and a DevWorks server exposing indexed code, planned work and Knowledge to other harnesses. Manage outgoing configurations, selected tools and incoming client access.'],
 ['DEC 16','Settings must be a substantial navigable workspace covering routing, MCP, GitHub, jobs, CodeGraph operations, database indexing connections, and agent tool access.'],
 ['DEC 17','Graph and database schema navigation, repository health analysis, code review visibility, and indexing logs are required product surfaces.'],
 ['DEC 18','Conventions must be managed without requiring a wiki interface. Skills must be supported and manageable.'],
 ['DEC 19','Downloadable managed executable language packs add optional .NET, TypeScript, Swift and Rust semantic indexing without rebuilding the app. Settings includes a dedicated Add-ons management screen.']])
 arch=find('4 ')
 arch['intro']='The proposed architecture is a modular Rust application with an egui native UI and an asynchronous execution runtime. A shared application command and query layer serves UI actions and agent tools. Logical components do not imply separately installed services.'
 comp=next(b for b in arch['blocks'] if b[0]=='table')[2]
 comp[0][1]='egui chat and workspaces, graph and schema exploration, code review and repository health, intelligence management, notifications, comprehensive Settings, and cost reporting.'
 comp[5][1]='Git and GitHub integration, outgoing MCP connections and incoming DevWorks server access, local process and file access, optional language helpers, database schema extraction, and supported tool adapters.'
 request(arch,'ARCH-06','Confirmed','The shipped native UI must use egui. Existing HTML artifacts remain design references; selecting egui does not convert the product into a web UI.')
 ui=find('14 ')
 paragraph(ui,'The updated UI study 05 preserves the chat-first layout and existing workspaces while adding standalone issues, unified tagged Knowledge, repository and schema exploration, health/reviews, agents and resources, project artifacts, approvals, scheduled jobs, comprehensive Settings, and usage/cache reporting. Model selectors and file/image attachment interactions are represented in chat composers. All data, inference and external operations are simulated locally; this is a browser-based design reference for the future native egui product, not its implementation. Newly added layouts and proposed interaction details remain subject to user review. The requirements interview has resumed at the user’s request.')
 rows=next(b for b in ui['blocks'] if b[0]=='table')[2]
 rows[-1][1]='A full Settings workspace for routing, MCP servers, GitHub, jobs, CodeGraph operations, database connections, tool permissions, skills, and preferences; usage and costs remain a separate reporting screen.'
 rows.append(['Add-ons','Manage downloadable .NET, TypeScript, Swift and Rust semantic language packs from Settings; inspect installation, updates, effective enablement, prerequisites, compatibility and indexing diagnostics.'])
 rows.extend([['Graphs and schemas','Use the CodeGraph repository home and its navigation as the baseline: overview, nodes, health, reviews, internal code projects, node-type browsing, linked entities and source. Database schema navigation and additional graph views remain to be refined.'],['Repository health and code reviews','Inspect analysis results, findings, evidence, source revisions, review history, and related work; distinct from the knowledge conflict queue.'],['Conventions and skills','Manage reusable conventions and skills, scope and applicability, versions, activation, and how agents receive them. A wiki interface is not required.'],['Indexing diagnostics','Inspect operation status and detailed logs, failures, attempts, and recovery actions.']])
 git=find('12 ')
 git['blocks']=[('p','Auto-Merge and mandatory independent review default to on, with project and repository overrides. Auto-Merge off stops before merge. Configured GitHub automated reviews satisfy mandatory review; repositories without working automation use an independent in-app reviewer. Both sources feed the fix cycle, and every actionable finding must be resolved. Deployments and release publishing have independent inherited policies under DELIVERY-09, both defaulting to Ask me. Force-push authority follows GIT-13: automatic only for agent-owned task branches, approval for feature branches and never main. Review-source discovery and completion follow the architecture contract; native qualification, other destructive Git authority and protected-branch enforcement remain implementation work.') if b[0]=='p' and b[1].startswith('The user wants almost fully autonomous delivery') else b for b in git['blocks']]
 plat=find('17 ')
 plat['blocks']=[b for b in plat['blocks'] if not (b[0]=='p' and b[1].startswith('Native toolkit selection must'))]
 paragraph(plat,'egui is selected based on the user’s positive experience with the artcraft projects. Implementation must still validate long streaming transcripts, rich text and code, large boards, graph and schema exploration, editable settings, accessible controls, diff rendering, pane resizing, clipboard, shortcuts, and packaging on the three platforms. Those checks validate the selected toolkit rather than reopen its selection.')

 settings=sec('18 Settings integrations and tool access','Settings becomes a full navigable workspace rather than a small modal. It must make a broad configuration surface manageable without occupying the main chat during ordinary work.')
 matrix(settings,['Topic','Required management surface'],[
 ['Router','Codex, Claude, OpenRouter and generic OpenAI-compatible endpoints; provider sign-in/keys, catalogs, routing policies, optional per-feature spending limits with warnings at 75%, supported prompt-cache controls and diagnostics. Usage/cost and cache-efficiency reporting remain separate.'],
 ['MCP','Outgoing servers and tool selection; incoming DevWorks server enablement, stdio/HTTP setup, per-client project/worktree scopes, read selection, explicit command grants, revocation and diagnostics.'],
 ['GitHub','One GitHub.com account, personal and organization repositories, authorization, repository associations, PR/check/review access, inbound issue updates, paced automatic triage and verified-resolution publication, polling status and integration diagnostics.'],
 ['Scheduled jobs','App-level and project-level inference tasks with saved instructions/actions, plus maintenance jobs; schedules, targets, enabled state, run history, triggers, concurrency and failure policy.'],
 ['CodeGraph operations','Manual discovery, indexing, schema synchronization, selected analysis, graph maintenance, and other retained operations.'],
 ['Database sources','Connections/file paths, schema indexing scope, freshness and errors; separate configurable read/write access for agent data tools. These target databases are distinct from app storage.'],
 ['Agent instructions','Inspect and edit instructions for workspace coordinators, feature coordinators, workers, reviewers and dreaming agents.'],
 ['Agent tools','Shared built-in/MCP selection, effective access for every agent, mandatory read-only reviewer restriction, and availability diagnostics.'],
 ['Conventions and skills','Shared skills in ~/.agents/skills and a dedicated harness skills directory; creation, management, scope, activation, version, provenance, and agent applicability.'],
 ['Containers','Choose an app-wide default and project overrides for Docker, Podman or Apple container on supported Macs; inspect installation, engine availability, configuration, capabilities and diagnostics.'],
 ['UI automation','Browser and desktop testing capabilities, backend/runtime availability, OS permissions, target selection, resource reservations, diagnostics and desktop-sharing policy.'],
 ['Add-ons','Dedicated screen for available and installed managed executable language packs; install/update/enable/disable/uninstall, versions, project enablement, configuration, prerequisites, compatibility, health and indexing diagnostics.'],
 ['General and diagnostics','Notifications, keep-computer-awake option, resource and per-feature/per-issue subagent limits, artifact storage and retention, backup, appearance, and local diagnostic controls.']],[1.2,5.7])
 request(settings,'SET-01','Confirmed','Settings must expand into a substantial categorized screen covering the required topics. It must remain accessible from the existing navigation while keeping chat as the opening interface.')
 request(settings,'SET-02','Proposed','Settings must provide discoverable categories and search, show configuration scope and effective values, validate edits, preserve unsaved changes, and distinguish configured values from connection health or runtime availability. Exact subnavigation and placement remain design decisions.')
 request(settings,'SET-03','Confirmed','Settings must let the user inspect, edit and save instructions for every agent role, including workspace coordinator, feature coordinator, worker, code reviewer and dreaming. Edited instructions must remain subject to harness-enforced policies, including mandatory read-only reviewers; prompt edits cannot grant tools or override those policies. Active agents adopt edits at their next safe step under SET-04; repository instructions take precedence over default roles under user directions and enforced permissions.')
 request(settings,'SET-04','Confirmed','Running agents must adopt user-saved or approved edits to their role instructions at their next safe step, without requiring a new session or manual restart. Unapproved agent proposals must not take effect. Retain the instructions used for completed work and preserve effective tool restrictions, user directions and unresolved blockers.')
 request(settings,'SET-05','Confirmed','Agents may draft changes to their role instructions, but explicit user approval is required before applying or activating those changes. Preserve version history and provide rollback. Approved changes reach active agents at their next safe step under SET-04; pending or rejected proposals leave the current instructions effective. Approval does not override user directions or configured tool permissions.')
 request(settings,'SET-06','Proposed','Stage agent-authored skill and role-instruction proposals separately from active files, using the approval queue and notifications. Show the affected scope, file changes, rationale, agent/work provenance and validation. Bind approval to the reviewed revision; changed proposals or concurrent user edits require reconciliation. Record approval/rejection and application outcomes. Apply only approved changes, retain prior versions, and keep unrelated authorized work running while approval is pending.')
 request(settings,'SET-07','Confirmed','Provide an app-wide preferred base directory for local repositories in Settings, with an optional override per project. Projects without an override inherit the app-wide default. Users must be able to choose their preferred repository location rather than rely on a fixed application path.')
 request(settings,'SET-08','Proposed','Provide a directory chooser and editable path, persist the repository base directory and report invalid or unavailable locations. Use it as the default destination for new repository clones while retaining support for repositories located elsewhere. Changing the setting must not move existing repositories or alter their recorded paths. Keep this setting distinct from harness artifact storage and worktree placement. Show the effective directory and whether it is inherited or overridden. Default path and clone-destination naming remain open.')
 request(settings,'CONTAINER-01','Confirmed','Provide an app-wide preferred container-system setting with optional per-project overrides, supporting Docker and Podman for projects that require containers. Projects without an override inherit the app-wide backend; clearing an override restores inheritance. Harness tools must honor the selected backend for container operations rather than require Docker for all projects. Container runtimes are optional project dependencies, not prerequisites for starting or using the harness itself.')
 request(settings,'CONTAINER-02','Confirmed','Offer Apple’s first-party container tooling as an additional backend on supported Macs where feasible. Detect supported OS/CPU and installed tooling and clearly show unavailable or unsupported states. Do not describe it as a universally preinstalled macOS capability. Rust host code may integrate with the optional external tool; no Swift development toolchain is required merely by choosing this backend. Validate the supported operation set before claiming parity with Docker or Podman.')
 request(settings,'CONTAINER-03','Proposed','Use backend adapters for image build/pull, container create/start/stop, command execution, inspection and logs, reporting supported operations explicitly. Show selected versus effective backend, executable/connection, version, health and errors in Settings. Record project/work/agent attribution, coordinate shared ports and other resources, and enforce existing tool permissions and read-only reviewer restrictions. Require normal system-install approval for installing runtimes. Do not silently substitute another backend or assume OCI-image compatibility guarantees Compose, networking, volume or Docker API compatibility. Closing the app stops agent-work containers under CONTAINER-04. Initial backend default, multi-service support, ownership/recovery mechanics and container lifecycle across pause, archive and deletion remain open.')
 request(settings,'CONTAINER-04','Confirmed','When the app closes, stop containers launched for agent work. Leave pre-existing and manually managed containers running. Preserve attribution so shutdown targets the agent-work containers rather than stopping the selected engine or all of its containers. Stopping is not permission to delete containers, images, volumes or stored data. Exact ownership detection, stop failure/recovery and restart behavior remain to specify.')
 paragraph(settings,'Container reference checked 1 November 2026: Apple container requires a separate signed installer and Apple silicon; its current README supports macOS 26. It runs OCI Linux images in lightweight VMs: https://github.com/apple/container . Podman uses managed VMs on macOS/Windows: https://docs.podman.io/en/latest/markdown/podman-machine.1.html . Podman Compose invokes an external provider: https://docs.podman.io/en/latest/markdown/podman-compose.1.html . Exact release/platform capability compatibility must be verified during implementation.')
 request(settings,'POWER-01','Confirmed','Settings must provide a keep-awake option, off by default, on macOS, Windows and Linux. While enabled and the app is open, prevent idle system sleep even without active work, but allow the display to turn off. Closing the app ends its keep-awake request.')
 request(settings,'POWER-02','Proposed','Use the operating system power-management facility to request prevention of idle system sleep. Show the configured value and whether the request is effective or unavailable; disabling the option releases the request. Do not imply that it prevents explicit sleep, lid-close behavior, shutdown, or every OS power policy. The setting defaults to off and allows display sleep under POWER-01.')
 request(settings,'MCP-01','Confirmed','Support both MCP directions: connect to external MCP servers alongside first-class built-in tools, and expose a DevWorks MCP server for other clients and harnesses. Users manage external server configurations and select the capabilities available to DevWorks agents. The earlier client-only decision is superseded.')
 request(settings,'MCP-02','Proposed','The supported transport and capability matrix must be explicit. Configure app-launched local processes and remote server connections where supported, with executable/arguments or endpoint, environment, working directory, authentication references, connection testing, start/stop or reconnect, capability discovery, and failure diagnostics.')
 request(settings,'MCP-03','Proposed','MCP tools must retain server identity and schemas, avoid name collisions, follow the same access policy and attribution rules as built-in tools, and link invocation inputs, results, failures, timeouts, and external effects to the responsible agent session and work.')
 request(settings,'MCP-04','Proposed','External servers may need optional runtimes such as Node or Python. The app must show those dependencies and failures explicitly without making them prerequisites for launching or using core functionality. Missing or unhealthy servers must not stall unrelated work.')
 request(settings,'MCP-05','Proposed','Tool discovery, server capability changes, resources and prompts where supported, and any server-initiated inference requests need an explicit compatibility and authority policy. Inference initiated through this integration must still follow the application router. This requirement governs outgoing client integration; incoming clients use the DevWorks server boundary under MCP-07–11.')
 request(settings,'MCP-06','Confirmed','When an MCP server is added, enable its read-only tools by default. Users must be able to choose which tools are available through individual toggles and an easy Select all control. Automatic enablement applies to read-only tools; other tools become available through user selection. Show the effective selection in Settings.')
 request(settings,'MCP-07','Confirmed','Other MCP-compatible clients and harnesses must be able to use DevWorks indexed code, planned work and Knowledge. Preserve code search, architecture/dependency/source retrieval, plans/features/issues/tasks and their histories, and Knowledge claims/evidence/conventions/document retrieval with the same scope, freshness and provenance as first-class application tools.')
 request(settings,'MCP-08','Proposed','Provide native stdio and Streamable HTTP access through a server adapter to the shared application gateway. Stdio uses a small installed bridge to the running desktop app; HTTP is opt-in and loopback-bound. Closing the app makes the server unavailable and must not leave a daemon or a second store writer. Publish version-qualified setup instructions and an explicit protocol/capability compatibility matrix.')
 request(settings,'MCP-09','Proposed','Incoming clients require individually authorized, revocable identities and explicit project/repository/worktree scopes. Default grants allow selected reads only. Human configuration controls connection approval, credential provisioning, tool/resource visibility and any write grants. Check current authority before invocation and publication; protocol metadata, client labels, tool annotations and resource URIs cannot grant authority. Credentials and private application/package transports are never exposed.')
 request(settings,'MCP-10','Proposed','Allow explicitly selected work and Knowledge mutations through existing typed commands, revisions, approvals, attribution, idempotency receipts and recovery rules. An external harness cannot impersonate a human, self-grant permissions, bypass reviewer restrictions or clear human-only execution holds. Index refresh or inference is an explicit separately granted command, never a hidden effect of context retrieval. Keep external inference and provider credentials owned by the external harness unless it explicitly invokes an authorized DevWorks operation.')
 request(settings,'MCP-11','Proposed','Expose discoverable tools and scoped resources, with optional context prompts, bounded pagination/results and precise unsupported or unavailable outcomes. Preserve view/source identity, citations, coverage gaps and work revisions; support cancellation and durable status/receipt recovery after disconnection without duplicate effects. Qualify at least two independent clients across both transports, including revoked scopes, isolated overlays, denied writes and restart behavior before advertising compatibility.')

 request(settings,'GH-01','Confirmed','The app must provide GitHub configuration and integration as a supported product capability, with visible account/host configuration, repository associations, authorization status, and access to linked PRs, checks, and code reviews.')
 request(settings,'GH-02','Proposed','GitHub credentials, token scopes and failures must be inspectable without exposing secrets. Git transport credentials and GitHub API authorization must be distinguished. Use the single GitHub.com account and organization support under GH-03. Use direct API polling under GH-10. Authentication method and additional Git hosts remain open; multiple-account and Enterprise support are not required by the agreed scope.')

 request(settings,'GH-03','Confirmed','Support one GitHub.com account, including personal repositories and repositories owned by organizations the account can access. Organization-owned repositories must support discovery, project association and the same authorized PR, check, review and delivery workflows as personal repositories. Preserve repository owner identity and respect actual account and repository permissions; organization support must not require signing in as another user.')
 request(settings,'GH-04','Confirmed','Always display references to GitHub resources as clickable links to the corresponding resource. Apply this across coordinator/agent messages, task and feature details, source-control views, activity/history, reviews, notifications and other app surfaces. This includes repositories, branches, commits, issues, PRs, reviews and checks/workflow runs when referenced. Use identifiable labels with repository/owner context where needed; a bare resource number, name or hash must not substitute for the link.')
 request(settings,'GH-05','Confirmed','For linked GitHub issues, automatically reflect remote updates in local tracking. Keep intermediate execution details local. Once work is completed and verified, automatically post a resolution summary and close the GitHub issue. Verify and use clarification about the same bug; genuinely new scope or feature requests require a user decision. Remote closure does not stop a verified bug already being fixed or approved/planned feature work. Close the local copy of an unstarted unreproducible bug; retain feature requests for manual decision. Preserve identity and source links; locally created issues stay local until explicitly published/linked.')
 request(settings,'GH-06','Proposed','Persist remote identity, observations, routed local work, import checkpoints and publication receipts. Deduplicate import/triage, coalesce updates and separate remote fields from local workflow, plan, pauses, budgets and private history. Use durable paced intake and outbox effects with echo suppression, bounded retries and observation-first recovery. Resolution publication binds verified completion/integration evidence; uncertain comment/close outcomes are reconciled before retry. Show queued/running/failed/conflicted state and authentication/rate-limit failures. Reopening a remote issue cannot clear a human pause or authorize resumed work.')
 request(settings,'GH-07','Confirmed','Agents verify and incorporate GitHub clarification about the same bug. Genuinely new scope or feature requests are held for a user decision with an in-app notification. Agents reconcile other conflicting updates automatically, retaining both versions, provenance and rationale. If unresolved, preserve both versions and escalate through the approval queue with an in-app notification. Only conflicting changes and affected work wait; unrelated work continues. Reconciliation cannot override user directions, permissions or pauses, or publish intermediate local execution details.')
 request(settings,'GH-08','Confirmed','Locally created issues stay local until explicitly published to GitHub or linked to an existing GitHub issue. Creation/editing never implicitly publishes or links them. After explicit linking/publication, apply inbound updates and verified-resolution publication under GH-05; retain local identity/history and configured permissions. Do not mirror every local edit or workflow status to GitHub.')
 request(settings,'GH-09','Confirmed','Automatically import existing open GitHub issues when connecting a repository and every newly reported issue thereafter. Queue automatic agent triage app-wide, admitting at most one issue every 30 minutes by default, with the interval configurable in Settings. Keep waiting issues visible without starting agents or catch-up bursts. Triage bug reports versus feature requests, verify reported bugs and immediately start verifiable bugs under existing execution controls. Notify in-app for feature requests or unverifiable bugs. Shared repositories have no single issue-owning project: triage routes incoming reports to relevant project context. Projects retain their own issues/features; feature coordinators and the message board coordinate overlapping work.')
 request(settings,'GH-10','Confirmed','Use direct GitHub API polling from the Rust application to discover relevant repository, push, PR/merge, issue, review and check/workflow changes. Use authenticated conditional requests where supported, respecting GitHub rate limits, retry guidance and endpoint poll-interval headers. No webhook receiver, public endpoint, tunnel or relay is required. Poll while the app is open and reconcile remote state after reopening, sleep/wake or connectivity recovery. Remote-state reconciliation must not replay missed scheduled inference runs or implicitly resume paused work.')
 request(settings,'GH-11','Confirmed','Changes discovered by GitHub polling must produce application events used both to notify relevant agents/coordinators and to trigger appropriate repository indexing updates. Events must retain their source repository and relevant work/PR context so notifications and index changes can be inspected alongside the affected work. Code-affecting changes such as pushes and merges must cause the affected indexed code to be refreshed; issue/review-only changes must not require a code reindex. Preserve source revision and visible index freshness.')
 request(settings,'GH-12','Proposed','Persist observed GitHub state and deduplicated application events in the existing SQLite runtime, with separate delivery/progress state for agent notification and indexing consumers. Repeated polls/restarts must not duplicate agent notices or indexing side effects. Deliver events to the responsible agent sessions as inspectable external updates without replacing user directions or clearing pauses; show undelivered updates. For code events, reconcile the affected configured ref/revision with remote content before incremental indexing, coalesce redundant pending refreshes and expose queued/running/failed/stale state and logs. An index failure must not suppress the corresponding agent event. Define initial baselines, event ordering, polling cadence, indexed-ref scope and replay/recovery mechanics before implementation.')
 request(settings,'DELIVERY-01','Confirmed','Settings must provide an explicit Auto-Merge on/off control, enabled by default with project and repository overrides. The target autonomous workflow supports committing, pushing, responding to reviews, fixing findings, and merging without routine human intervention. With Auto-Merge off, the workflow must stop before merge while other authorized work may continue.')
 request(settings,'DELIVERY-02','Confirmed','Mandatory independent code review before merge defaults to on, with project/repository overrides and a separate-agent fix/review cycle. Configured GitHub automated reviews normally satisfy it. Use an independent in-app reviewer when automation is absent, fails or never completes. Automation failure must not indefinitely prevent delivery; fallback still requires complete review and resolution of every actionable finding.')
 request(settings,'DELIVERY-03','Confirmed','Use configured GitHub review automation normally; automatically fall back to an independent in-app reviewer if it fails or never completes. Avoid duplicate fallback launches. Bind review evidence and findings to the exact current head and source identity, including explicit completion with zero findings. Keep actionable external findings in the fix cycle even if fallback is used. Fallback satisfies the DevWorks review requirement without bypassing required checks, permissions or branch protection. Changed heads need current review evidence; all actionable findings must be resolved before merge.')
 request(settings,'DELIVERY-04','Confirmed','Auto-Merge and mandatory-review policy must be configurable per repository with inherited defaults from project and workspace settings. Auto-Merge and mandatory independent review both default to on; project settings may override each inherited value, and repository settings may override each project value. The effective value and source of each value, including local overrides, must be visible. One repository’s review setup must not be assumed to apply to every repository in its project.')
 request(settings,'DELIVERY-05','Confirmed','Every actionable review finding must be resolved before an automatic merge, including minor findings. A severity threshold alone must not permit unresolved actionable findings. The feature coordinator adjudicates disputed or non-actionable findings, recording its rationale in the linked review history. Findings it judges actionable must still be fixed.')
 request(settings,'DELIVERY-06','Proposed','Merge readiness must reconcile the current code revision, required checks, the review source selected under DELIVERY-02–03, all actionable findings, effective policy, and cross-repository dependencies. Apply the effective repository review and Auto-Merge policy to both task/group PRs into the feature branch and the final feature PR into main. Review findings, fixes, re-reviews, dismissals and reasons must remain linked to the feature, PR, revision, and participating agents.')
 request(settings,'DELIVERY-07','Confirmed','The maximum number of fix/review cycles must be configurable in Settings, with a default of 3. Execution must honor the configured limit rather than continue an unbounded fix loop. Reaching the limit does not waive the requirement to resolve every actionable finding before merge.')
 request(settings,'DELIVERY-08','Confirmed','If the feature coordinator cannot reach a decision, it must raise the issue to the user and publish an in-app notification. This applies to unresolved review disputes or fix-cycle outcomes it cannot resolve. The escalation must retain the relevant findings, attempts, evidence, and coordinator rationale; unresolved actionable findings continue to block that merge. Escalation must not pause independent feature work: only the affected task and its dependent work wait.')
 request(settings,'DELIVERY-09','Confirmed','Provide separate settings for agent-initiated deployments and release publishing. Each has Automatic, Ask me and Disabled values and defaults to Ask me. Inherit workspace defaults through project settings, with independent per-repository overrides; show effective values and their sources. These controls are independent of each other and of Auto-Merge. Ask me requires explicit user approval before the action; Disabled prevents it; Automatic permits it subject to existing tool permissions and applicable execution rules. A merge is not deployment or release approval. Harness self-improvement installation/restart still requires explicit approval under SELF-03.')
 request(settings,'TOOL-01','Confirmed','Users must be able to manage which tools agents can access, primarily through Settings. Management must cover both built-in application tools and tools supplied by MCP servers.')
 request(settings,'TOOL-02','Proposed','The effective tool set must be inspectable for an agent/session, derived from the shared selection under TOOL-04 and the mandatory reviewer restriction under TOOL-05. MCP defaults follow MCP-06. Shell/process and web defaults follow TOOL-06 and TOOL-08. Define remaining built-in defaults, denial precedence, and effects on queued and active runs. Disabled tools must be enforced at invocation time rather than merely omitted from prompts.')
 request(settings,'TOOL-03','Proposed','Record tool policy revisions and changes with actor attribution. Tool descriptions and schemas are not authority to exceed user policy. Preserve explicit user selections during tool discovery. Define how read-only classification is established, how missing or conflicting metadata is handled, and how later tool additions or capability changes affect saved selections. Initial MCP defaults and selection controls follow MCP-06.')

 request(settings,'TOOL-04','Confirmed','The configured tool selection applies to every agent, rather than requiring separate selections for coordinators, coding agents, and reviewers. Code-review agents must have access to the same selected read tools as the other agents.')
 request(settings,'TOOL-05','Confirmed','Code-review agents must always be read-only. The harness must deny write/edit and other mutating operations, including indirect writes through shell/process tools, while preserving the shared selected read tools. Enforce this for built-in and MCP invocations, not only in reviewer instructions; Settings and Select all must not override it. The harness retains review findings and history without granting the reviewer general write access. Fixes belong to separately authorized implementation agents.')
 request(settings,'TOOL-06','Confirmed','Enable built-in shell/process tools by default for implementation agents, subject to configured tool permissions and project scope. Users may disable them in Settings. Default enablement must not grant review agents write access or bypass TOOL-05.')
 request(settings,'TOOL-07','Confirmed','Implementation agents may install project dependencies automatically within authorized project/worktree scope. System-wide tool or package installations require user approval before execution. Configured permissions and read-only reviewer restrictions still apply. Record installation actions and approval outcomes in the associated work history.')
 request(settings,'TOOL-08','Confirmed','Provide built-in web search and page-fetching tools, including documentation retrieval, enabled by default. Apply shared tool selection and invocation permissions, including access for read-only review agents. Retain tool activity, source URLs and results in the responsible agent/work history.')

 explore=sec('19 Graph schema health and review workspaces','Code intelligence must have human-facing exploration and management surfaces, not only agent tools. The existing CodeGraph repository home and its navigation are the accepted reference for repository exploration, implemented natively with egui. Database schema navigation and any additional graph-specific views still require detail.')
 request(explore,'GRAPH-01','Confirmed','The app must provide a way to navigate code graphs and their relationships, move between entities and their source context, and inspect graph information within repository and project scope.')
 request(explore,'GRAPH-02','Confirmed','Follow the existing CodeGraph repository home and navigation as the baseline for repository exploration. Opening a repository leads to its Overview, with Nodes, Health, and Reviews available directly. Preserve repository identity and indexed revision/freshness, summary and confidence, health and activity indicators, internal code-project analysis, inbound/outbound dependencies, node-type counts and drill-down, and README where available. The workspace coordinator chat remains the application opening screen.')
 request(explore,'GRAPH-03','Confirmed','Preserve the CodeGraph drill-down from a repository or internal code project into a node list, optionally filtered by node type, with grouped node kinds, expandable containers and members, and links to node details. Node details retain qualified name, properties, trust/confidence, source location and line-numbered source, and incoming/outgoing relationships grouped by type. Related nodes and cross-repository relationships must be navigable, with breadcrumbs back to repository and node-type context.')
 request(explore,'GRAPH-04','Proposed','Adapt the accepted navigation flow to egui and the multi-repository project model without copying Angular routing or web hosting. Internal code projects/modules must be distinguished from top-level work projects that group repositories. Preserve repository context across home, list and detail views; provide return navigation to the originating workspace. Extra graph visualization controls and saved views remain open rather than replacing the accepted repository home.')
 request(explore,'DB-01','Confirmed','Database indexing must be supported alongside repository indexing. The app must manage database indexing sources and connections, including a file path for SQLite or another supported file-backed database.')
 request(explore,'DB-02','Proposed','A database source must declare engine/adapter, display identity, project associations and effective access, file path or connection endpoint, credential references where needed, enabled state, schema selection, and indexing status. Reuse one connection definition across project associations under DB-09. Creating, editing, testing, disabling, reindexing, and removing indexed metadata must be available without deleting or modifying the source database.')
 request(explore,'DB-03','Proposed','Schema indexing must use read-only source access and extract supported tables, columns, types, keys, indexes, views, procedures and relationships. Report coverage per adapter. Indexing does not read row contents into a searchable data index. Agent queries and data mutations are separate operations under DB-04–05.')
 request(explore,'DB-04','Confirmed','Database indexing covers schemas only and remains read-only. Separately provide agent tools to read and manipulate data. Each database connection defaults to read-only; data writes require explicit user enablement for that connection. Enforce connection policy together with configured tool permissions and project scope. Enabling writes on one connection must not enable them on others. Code-review agents remain read-only even when connection writes are enabled.')
 request(explore,'DB-05','Proposed','Expose database data tools against configured target connections, with visible effective read/write permissions and project scope. Support queries and permitted data inserts, updates and deletes, with adapter-specific capability reporting, execution/result limits, cancellation and attributed history. Enforce read-only access at the execution/connection boundary where supported, including indirect mutations; SQL keyword checks alone are insufficient. Under DB-08, link schema changes to their migration commit or direct operation, recording the fallback reason and outcome. Define transaction, failure/retry and sensitive-result handling without importing row data into the schema index.')
 request(explore,'DB-06','Confirmed','Support SQLite, MariaDB/MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQL Server as target database families for schema indexing and separately permissioned data tools. MariaDB and MySQL must both be covered and verified, with differences reported. These are target connections, independent of the selected SQLite and sqlite-vec internal app storage. Supported engine versions, authentication modes and metadata coverage remain to be qualified. HelixDB and HydraDB are excluded from target readers.')
 request(explore,'DB-07','Confirmed','Do not implement a HelixDB schema reader or structural-inference adapter. The user removed this conditional capability on 2 November 2026. Retain historical storage evaluations only as evidence; they neither enable HelixDB connections nor add an internal storage dependency. Schema indexing for the selected relational targets remains metadata-only, with separately permissioned data tools governed by DB-04–06.')
 request(explore,'DB-08','Confirmed','Agents may change database schemas, including supported CREATE, ALTER and DROP operations. Prefer migration files committed to the appropriate project repository through its normal worktree, review and delivery workflow. If committed migrations are not possible, direct schema changes are allowed. Applying either route requires writes to be explicitly enabled for the target connection and the agent to have the necessary tool permissions and project scope. A migration commit alone does not authorize application to a database. Indexing and code-review agents remain read-only. Preserve the associated task, agent and action history.')
 request(explore,'DB-09','Confirmed','Database connections may be shared across multiple projects, with access controlled per project. Sharing a connection must not automatically grant access to other projects or their agents. Resolve the requesting project and enforce its access together with the connection settings and agent tool permissions on every operation. Project access cannot enable writes while the connection remains read-only. Indexing and code-review agents remain read-only even where project and connection settings permit writes. Retain project and responsible-agent attribution for operations against shared connections.')
 request(explore,'SCHEMA-01','Confirmed','Users must be able to navigate indexed schemas, inspect their objects and relationships, and understand their connection to code or project work. File-backed database sources must not require an application storage server.')
 request(explore,'SCHEMA-02','Proposed','Schema exploration should support object search, table/column details, keys and constraints, relationship traversal, source identity, sync freshness and failures, and code-to-schema references where evidence supports them. Unsupported metadata must not appear as confirmed absent.')
 request(explore,'HEALTH-01','Confirmed','Repository health analysis must be visible in the UI, with access to analysis results and the findings that need attention. This is distinct from the health of the app, its stores, or its background workers.')
 request(explore,'HEALTH-02','Proposed','Health results must identify repository/revision, analysis time, coverage, categories and severity where supported, evidence, trends where available, and linked corrective work. Missing, stale, partial, or failed analysis must be distinguished from a healthy result. Scoring and remediation automation remain open.')
 request(explore,'CODEREVIEW-01','Confirmed','Code reviews must have a visible management and inspection surface, linked to repositories, features, source changes, PRs, and the agents or users who produced and addressed the findings.')
 request(explore,'CODEREVIEW-02','Proposed','Review views must distinguish local agent reviews from GitHub reviews, support findings and their status, severity, source/diff locations, responses and revisions, and show whether findings remain applicable to the current code. Knowledge conflict approvals must not be mixed into code review state.')

 practice=sec('20 Conventions and skills','Conventions and skills are required managed inputs to agent behavior. Conventions express reusable guidance and expectations; skills package instructions or procedures for a task. Harness self-improvement is an explicit product goal, including agent-proposed improvements to reusable harness-specific skills and role instructions, applied with user approval. Boundaries with lessons and role instructions must remain explicit.')
 request(practice,'CONV-01','Confirmed','Users must be able to manage conventions. The app need not reproduce the CodeGraph wiki interface; a dedicated searchable conventions manager is sufficient if it preserves the required behavior.')
 request(practice,'CONV-02','Proposed','Conventions must support creation and editing, search, scope and applicability, activation or retirement, versions, source provenance, and agent retrieval. Project-specific conventions remain scoped to their project under KNOW-07. A session must be able to identify the convention revisions it received. Storage/import format and synchronization with existing sources remain open.')
 request(practice,'SKILL-01','Confirmed','The harness must support skills and provide a way to manage them. Skills must be discoverable to the relevant coordinators and workers rather than existing only as hidden files or duplicated prompt text.')
 request(practice,'SKILL-02','Proposed','Skill management should support discovery/import, inspection, creation/editing of local skills, enable/disable, scope, version/source identity, and validation of referenced instructions and assets. Skills are file-backed in the shared and harness-specific directories under SKILL-04. Harness-specific skills take precedence over shared skills with the same name. Referenced documents, scripts and assets are supported under SKILL-06. Refine format compatibility, reference resolution, dependency detection and helper invocation mechanics.')
 request(practice,'SKILL-03','Proposed','A session must record selected skills and their revisions. Skills must obey tool access and user authority, preserve references to lessons they address, and make conflicts with conventions, rules, or role instructions visible. Record skill-version transitions in session history. Apply changes only after in-flight work reaches a safe boundary; do not retroactively rewrite the instructions or evidence for completed steps.')

 request(practice,'SKILL-04','Confirmed','Support using existing shared skill folders from ~/.agents/skills and a separate harness-specific skills directory. Users must be able to create harness-specific skills in that dedicated directory and manage skills from both locations in the app. Shared skills must be usable from their existing location rather than requiring an import copy. Show each skill’s origin, filesystem location, and which version is active. Harness-specific skills take precedence over shared skills with the same name. The exact harness-specific directory path remains open.')

 request(practice,'SKILL-05','Confirmed','When a skill edit is user-saved or approved, running agents using that skill must adopt the updated version at their next safe step, without requiring a new session or manual restart. Unapproved agent proposals must not take effect. Existing tool-access policy, explicit user instructions, and unresolved blockers remain effective.')
 request(practice,'SKILL-06','Confirmed','Support skills containing referenced documents, executable scripts and assets alongside their instructions, in both shared and harness-specific skill directories. Agents must be able to access these resources and invoke permitted scripts. Scripts use normal harness tool permissions and dependency rules; a skill must not grant additional authority or bypass disabled tools, reviewer read-only restrictions, project scope or approval for system-wide installation. Optional skill runtime requirements must remain explicit and must not become prerequisites for core app use.')
 request(practice,'SKILL-07','Confirmed','Agents may draft new harness-specific skills and edits to their own skills, supporting harness self-improvement. Explicit user approval is required before creating or updating the active skill or making the proposed version available to agents. Record the responsible agent, work/session, rationale, revisions and approval outcome in visible history. Existing tool permissions, user directions, dependency rules and next-safe-step adoption still apply. This supersedes the earlier policy allowing automatic skill edits without approval. Agent improvements to shared skills use approved harness-specific overrides under SKILL-09. Harness-code improvements follow SELF-01.')
 request(practice,'SKILL-08','Proposed','Preserve prior skill versions and show changes, rationale and supporting evidence in the skill manager and linked work history. Provide revision-aware rollback without overwriting later edits silently. Record validation performed and its outcome; an agent’s claim that a skill is improved must not be represented as demonstrated effectiveness. Define validation and recovery mechanics while preserving the required user approval for agent-authored skill changes under SKILL-07.')
 request(practice,'SKILL-09','Confirmed','For an agent-proposed improvement to a shared skill in ~/.agents/skills, propose a harness-specific override and leave the shared original and its files untouched. User approval is required before creating/updating the active override under SKILL-07. Pending or rejected proposals do not change the effective skill. Once approved, the harness-specific skill takes precedence over its same-name shared source, with active agents adopting it at the next safe step. Preserve source/override provenance and version history.')
 practice['blocks'].append(('h2','Harness code improvements'))
 request(practice,'SELF-01','Confirmed','The harness must be able to propose improvements to its own Rust code and handle them through the normal project feature, PR and review workflow. Use the same planning and approval rules, isolated worktrees, implementation, verification, review/fix cycle and configured merge policy as other project work. Retain linked work, agents, transcripts, Git actions, reviews and evidence. Self-improvement does not bypass configured permissions or delivery gates. Installing the resulting build and restarting the app require explicit approval under SELF-03.')
 request(practice,'SELF-02','Proposed','Represent the harness repository as ordinary managed project work. Distinguish the running application version from the source revision, tested candidate build and merged code; a successful merge must not imply the running app has updated. Link build artifacts and verification to the self-improvement feature/task. Implement approval-gated installation/restart under SELF-03. Refine restart preparation, checkpoint/resume, compatibility and rollback mechanics, preserving other work and existing shutdown/resume policies.')
 request(practice,'SELF-03','Confirmed','Installing a self-improvement build and restarting the app to activate it require explicit user approval. A merged PR or passing tests/reviews must not count as that approval. While approval is pending or denied, continue using the installed version; unrelated authorized work may continue. Applying an approved update must preserve existing shutdown and selective-resume rules. Record approval and update outcomes.')
 request(practice,'SELF-04','Confirmed','Every agent-authored harness self-improvement must include documentation as part of the work. Update the affected local Help, UI/tool behavior descriptions and developer/extension guides or examples to match the change, including changes to harness code, roles, skills and packages as applicable. Include documentation in the plan and acceptance criteria, deliver it with the change, and verify and review its accuracy and completeness. Missing, stale or incomplete required documentation blocks completion and delivery; it must not be deferred as optional follow-up work. Preserve existing approval and activation rules. Shipped documentation must match the installed version under HELP-05.')

 jobs=sec('21 Scheduled tasks operations and indexing diagnostics','Users need to schedule inference tasks at app and project scope, run maintenance jobs and manual operations, and understand failures. All use the app’s durable runtime, router policies and observable history while the desktop application is running.')
 request(jobs,'JOB-01','Confirmed','The app must manage scheduled background jobs, including the selected CodeGraph job types. Settings must expose schedules and controls, while run status and meaningful outcomes remain visible from related workspaces.')
 request(jobs,'JOB-02','Proposed','Job records must include type, target/scope, schedule and timezone, enabled state, parameters, last and next run, and linked execution history. Create/edit, pause/enable, Run now, inspect, and supported cancellation/retry controls must be available. Scheduled inference overlap is skipped under JOB-08. Define checkpoint/reconciliation mechanics, failure/backoff, resource controls and usage reporting without job spending limits under JOB-09; missed occurrences are skipped under JOB-03 and interrupted runs resume under JOB-04.')
 request(jobs,'JOB-03','Confirmed','Enabled maintenance and scheduled inference tasks at app/project scope reactivate automatically when the app reopens, independently of manual feature and issue resumption. Missed occurrences stay missed: compute the next future occurrence without replaying them, coalescing them into an immediate catch-up run or launching a startup catch-up batch. Runs already active when the app closed resume under JOB-04.')
 request(jobs,'JOB-04','Confirmed','Maintenance and scheduled inference runs active at app closure resume automatically on reopening, without waiting for their next due time or requiring feature or issue resumption. Continue the interrupted run with its existing identity, progress and history; this does not replay missed occurrences. Preserve separate user pauses, budget holds and approval waits. Job resumption must not implicitly resume feature or issue agents paused by app closure; those require manual Continue.')
 request(jobs,'JOB-05','Confirmed','Support scheduled inference tasks at both project level and app level. Each task must be configurable to run instructions and perform automated actions through the harness’s tools and router. A required use case is periodically checking a GitHub repository for newly reported issues and performing a configured automated action. Project tasks retain their project context; app-level tasks can cover configured work across projects. Scheduled actions remain subject to effective tool permissions and existing approval policies.')
 request(jobs,'JOB-06','Proposed','Manage each inference schedule’s name, scope/targets, saved prompt, schedule/timezone, enabled state, routing and usage reporting without job spending limits under JOB-09, with skipped inference overlap under JOB-08 and visible failure policy. Provide create/edit, enable/disable, Run now, inspect and cancel controls. Each run has a linked agent transcript, tool/action history, usage, outcome and created work/artifacts. Persist discovery checkpoints and action identities so repeated issue scans and retries do not duplicate actions. Unresolved actions enter the existing input queue with notifications. Apply no-server and pause/exit constraints, with automatic schedule/run resumption and no catch-up under JOB-03–04; reconcile external effects before retry.')
 request(jobs,'JOB-07','Confirmed','The agent launched by a scheduled inference task has the full configured tool access available to an authorized coordinator/implementation agent, including tools that select and start work. It is not restricted to monitoring or read-only actions merely because it was scheduled. It may call next_work(project, feature = null) to select a feature or standalone issue, or pass a feature to select one of its tasks, and start that work. Existing tool permissions, plan-readiness/approval rules and explicit pauses still apply; scheduled execution does not grant a policy bypass.')
 request(jobs,'JOB-08','Confirmed','If a scheduled inference task is still running when its next scheduled occurrence arrives, skip that occurrence. Do not start an overlapping run or queue that occurrence for later catch-up. Preserve the current run and expose the skipped outcome in schedule history. Existing restart, interruption and explicit-pause rules remain in effect.')
 request(jobs,'JOB-09','Confirmed','Scheduled inference jobs report per-run usage and costs but have no job spending limits or monetary-limit holds. Apply ROUTE-17 reported/estimated/free/untracked classifications. Feature work started by a schedule remains subject to that feature’s configured budget and normal approval, pause, eligibility and permission rules; invoking it through a job must not bypass those controls. Ordinary cancellation, deadlines and resource controls remain independent of monetary limits.')
 request(jobs,'OP-01','Confirmed','Retained CodeGraph operations must be manually invokable in the app. The user must not need to ask an agent or run the old service to perform them. Manual and scheduled invocation must use the same validated operations as agents where applicable.')
 request(jobs,'OP-02','Proposed','Inventory the retained operations and jobs before implementation scope is frozen. Candidate operations include repository discovery/indexing/reindexing, schema sync, selected analysis, graph linking/community detection, embeddings, and maintenance. Show prerequisites, target scope, progress, cancellation, actual outcome, and recovery actions.')
 request(jobs,'LOG-01','Confirmed','Indexing logs must be visible, including enough detail to diagnose failures. Repository, database-schema, and optional semantic indexing must not hide their errors behind a generic failed badge.')
 request(jobs,'LOG-02','Proposed','A run’s diagnostics must correlate operation, target, stage, source revision, attempt, timestamps, progress, warnings/errors, helper output where relevant, and error classification. Provide filtering/search, live inspection, retained run history, copy/export, and links to safe recovery actions while redacting credentials and controlling large output.')
 request(jobs,'LOG-03','Proposed','Partial indexing, stale data, cancelled runs, and terminal or retryable failures must be distinguishable. Logs and relevant artifacts must survive restart under a defined retention policy. Jobs run while the application is open, including while other screens are active. Closing the main window pauses work and exits. Feature and issue work require manual selection after closing and reopening; it continues automatically after sleep while the app remains open. Enabled maintenance/inference schedules reactivate automatically; missed occurrences stay skipped and interrupted runs resume automatically. Detailed recovery mechanics still need policy.')
 paragraph(jobs,'Indexed CodeGraph references confirm job types for repository discovery, community detection, convention embedding ingestion, and assistant retention cleanup. The route map also exposes schedules, manual operations, schemas and graph exploration. These references establish a starting inventory, not proof that every retained job or new target adapter is already ported.')
 jobs['blocks'].append(('h2','Initial CodeGraph data import'))
 request(jobs,'IMPORT-01','Confirmed','Start with fresh repository indexing rather than migrating the existing derived code index. Import existing CodeGraph projects, features, tasks and their histories. This is the complete selected migration scope; exclude existing memory, knowledge, lessons and conventions. Preserve the imported records and their historical relationships when rebuilding indexes. The exclusion concerns migration only; the new harness still provides its required Knowledge and convention management capabilities.')
 request(jobs,'IMPORT-02','Proposed','Inventory all records and relationships within the selected project, feature, task and history scope: repository associations, plans, priorities, statuses, dependencies, agent/session and transcript links, decisions and Git/PR/review history, with referenced evidence where present. Preserve source identities or durable ID mappings, attribution, timestamps and provenance. Use a consistent read-only snapshot/export, validate counts and links, and make import resumable without duplicates. Report missing data or mapping failures rather than silently dropping selected records. Preserve historical code references while reconciling links to fresh indexes. Import must not automatically resume old agents, execute queued effects or treat historical checks as current verification.')

 open_section=find('18 ');accept=find('19 ');refs=find('20 ')
 rows=next(b for b in open_section['blocks'] if b[0]=='table')[2]
 replacements={
 'OPEN 10':'Closing the main window pauses agents and background jobs and exits, with no tray continuation. On reopening, feature and issue agents stay paused until the user resumes selected features and issues, individually or together; unselected work remains paused. Enabled maintenance/inference schedules reactivate automatically with no catch-up; interrupted runs resume automatically. Independent pauses, budget holds and approval waits remain. Sleep/wake while the app stays open automatically continues previously running work. Keep-awake defaults to off and allows display sleep when enabled. Decide recovery mechanics, shutdown timeouts, and remaining control granularity.',
 'OPEN 06':'Concurrent subagent caps are independent per feature and per issue, configurable with a default of 10 each; no global agent-count cap applies. Maximize ready-task concurrency. Feature Start reviews planning readiness; incomplete feature plans require approval or input, and plans remain refinable. After plan approval, the coordinator may adapt execution autonomously under approved requirements and configured policies. Changes to approved scope or acceptance criteria require user approval; unblocked work valid under the approved plan continues. next_work(project, feature=null) selects the highest-priority eligible feature or standalone issue from one project queue; a supplied feature restricts selection to its tasks. Automatic feature selection requires implementation approval and skips incomplete/unapproved plans; explicit Start retains planning readiness. Standalone issues need no feature; their PRs merge directly to main. Clear, actionable issues may implement immediately without separate plan approval, track spending without a cap and delegate to subagents. Set claim recovery, priority tie-breaking and remaining execution/workflow rules. Custom Kanban columns and status names are confirmed per project; refine semantic mappings and transition rules.',
 'OPEN 05':'Auto-Merge and mandatory independent review both default to on, with project and repository overrides and a configurable fix cycle. Every actionable finding must be resolved. The feature coordinator adjudicates disputed findings with recorded rationale. The fix-cycle limit is configurable with a default of 3; an undecidable outcome escalates to the user with a notification. Configured GitHub automated reviews satisfy mandatory review; run an independent in-app reviewer where automation is absent, fails or never completes. Deployments and release publishing each use Automatic, Ask me or Disabled, defaulting to Ask me with workspace/project/repository inheritance, independently of Auto-Merge. Set configuration discovery/completion signals, exact cycle-counting semantics, deployment/release execution and approval mechanics, other destructive Git authority and remaining protected-branch handling. Force-push authority is settled under GIT-13: automatic on agent-owned task branches, approval for feature branches and never main; guarded execution details are proposed under GIT-14.',
 'OPEN 01':'egui is selected. Minimum OS targets are macOS 26.2 and Windows 10; determine the Linux distribution/version matrix during architecture validation. Set CPU architectures, egui/renderer integration versions, packaging and measurable footprint/responsiveness targets.',
 'OPEN 02':'Finish the retained CodeGraph capability inventory. Graphs, schemas, database indexing, health, code reviews, jobs/manual operations, conventions, skills, and logs are now required; refine their exact controls and remaining exclusions.',
 'OPEN 04':'Codex, Claude, OpenRouter and OpenAI-compatible endpoints use simple-router as reference. Refine protocols, cache support and accounting. Feature budgets are configured or Off; warnings start at 75%, and reached limits pause inference. Raising/removing a limit requires manual Continue. Define unknown-cost accounting, concurrent/in-flight reservations, warning cadence and defaults. GitHub.com requires one account and organization repositories. Linked issues synchronize edits/status both ways automatically; agents reconcile sync conflicts and escalate unresolved decisions with notifications. Issues created locally stay local until explicitly published or linked. Existing open GitHub issues import when a repository is connected, and newly reported issues import automatically. Direct API polling supplies events for agent notification and repository indexing. Refine poll cadence, field coverage, status mapping and linking/publication controls. Select authentication and any additional hosts.',
 'OPEN 12':'Rebuild repository indexes and import CodeGraph projects, features, tasks and their histories; exclude existing memory, knowledge, lessons and conventions. Finalize source mappings/extraction, repeatable import and historical reference reconciliation within that scope. Artifacts remain until explicitly deleted, with automatic cleanup disabled by default; backups are manual only. Set quotas, version/deletion and manual backup/export mechanics. MCP has outgoing client and incoming DevWorks server support; settle transport compatibility and access through the shared gateway.',
 'OPEN 13':'Required-input OS alerts when unfocused are confirmed where supported/permitted. Session pins survive app restarts; Auto resolves to a pinned model. Rate limits/unavailability trigger replacement and re-pinning; if all eligible models are cooling, wait until the earliest reported expiry and reconnect automatically. Refine defaults, catalog refresh, capability changes and recovery without availability times; Settings, other notification preferences, themes, pane sizing, session organization, updates and setup. New screens require visual design review.'}
 for row in rows:
  if row[0] in replacements:row[1]=replacements[row[0]]
 rows.extend([['OPEN 14','The CodeGraph repository home and its navigation are accepted for repository exploration: Overview, Nodes, Health, Reviews, internal code projects, node-type lists, node details, linked relationships and source. Refine native layout, database schema navigation, any additional graph visualization controls, and saved views.'],['OPEN 15','Database indexing is schema-only, with separate data tools; connections are shareable across projects with access controlled per project, default to read-only and require explicit per-connection write enablement. SQLite, MariaDB/MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQL Server are required targets; HelixDB schema reading is excluded by user decision. Schema changes prefer committed migrations; direct changes are allowed when migrations are not possible, under the same connection and agent permissions. Set engine versions, authentication, metadata coverage, migration execution, query/mutation and permission mechanics. HelixDB read-only record inspection to infer structure is approved, retaining schema metadata only and no source records/property values. Validate discovery coverage and read-only enforcement. Refine convention storage and context delivery. Shared skills use ~/.agents/skills; harness-specific skills are created in a dedicated directory. Harness-specific skills take precedence over same-name shared skills. Running agents adopt skill edits at their next safe step. Referenced documents, scripts and assets are confirmed, with scripts under normal tool/dependency rules. Set directory path, format compatibility, reference resolution, helper/dependency mechanics and safe boundaries and validation/rollback mechanics. Agent-authored skill creation/edits and role-instruction revisions require user approval before application, with history and safe-step adoption. Harness-code improvements use the normal feature/PR/review workflow. Installing self-improvement builds and restarting require explicit approval. Shared-skill improvements use approved harness-specific overrides, preserving originals. Refine override resource/version handling, update preparation, compatibility, checkpoint/resume and rollback mechanics. SQLite/file-based support remains required; target databases are separate from app storage.'],['OPEN 16','One shared tool selection applies to every agent, including the same read tools for reviewers. MCP tools default to read-only enabled, with individual toggles and Select all. Reviewers are always read-only, enforced by the harness. Implementation shell/process tools and shared web search/page fetching default to enabled. Define classification, later capability changes, remaining built-in defaults, and active-run updates. App/project scheduled inference tasks are required. Their schedules reactivate and interrupted runs resume automatically, with missed occurrences skipped. Scheduled agents have full configured tool access and can use next_work to start work, under existing permissions/approval rules. Scheduled inference overlap is skipped under JOB-08. Refine deduplication, usage reporting without job spending limits under JOB-09, log retention and health/review workflows.']])
 paragraph(open_section,'Interview decisions: delivery can commit, push, fix review findings, and merge autonomously under an Auto-Merge setting that defaults to on with project and repository overrides; mandatory review by a separate agent also defaults to on with project and repository overrides, and its fix cycle is configurable; configured GitHub automated reviews satisfy mandatory review, and in-app review runs when automation is absent, fails or never completes; policy is per repository with inherited defaults; every actionable finding must be resolved. The feature coordinator adjudicates disputed findings with a recorded rationale; actionable findings must still be fixed. The configurable fix-cycle limit defaults to 3. If the coordinator cannot reach a decision, it escalates to the user and publishes a notification.')
 rows.append(['OPEN 17','Browser and native desktop UI testing are required. Choose automation backends and runtime/browser provisioning, native platform capability coverage, desktop permissions, idle detection, approval lifetime/retry mechanics, isolation/test fixtures and resource scheduling. Non-idle takeover must ask with OK or Wait. Preserve the Rust core and no mandatory preinstalled Node/Python/.NET requirement.'])
 rows=next(b for b in accept['blocks'] if b[0]=='table')[2]
 rows.extend([
 ['Project workflows','Configure different custom columns and status names in two projects. Rename and reorder columns through UI and tools; each project retains its own workflow. Verify that status identities and history survive renaming and that allocation uses explicit readiness/completion semantics. Removing an occupied status requires explicit reassignment without lost work. Board movement must not clear execution holds or bypass approvals, reviews, checks or merge policy. WORK 02–03, 13–14; MODEL 03; WORK 09–10.'],
 ['GitHub polling and event consumers','With no webhook endpoint or relay, detect remote pushes, PR merges, issue updates, reviews and check changes through direct API polling. Verify attributed application events reach relevant agent sessions and code-affecting events refresh the correct configured ref/revision with visible index freshness. Issue/review-only changes do not reindex code. Exercise conditional responses, rate-limit backoff, duplicate polls, restart and offline/sleep recovery; notification and indexing delivery remain independent and traceable. Preserve manual work pauses and skipped scheduled runs. GH 10–12; CODE 04–05; CONTROL 08–11; JOB 03–04.'],
 ['Automatic GitHub issue import','Connect a repository with existing open GitHub issues and verify automatic import into the project issue list. Then report a new issue and verify automatic import without a manual action or inference schedule. Preserve the source owner/repository, remote identity and clickable link; repeated discovery, offline recovery and restart must reuse the same local issue. Verify inbound updates and one verified-resolution summary/close without publishing intermediate local tracking. Check the configurable app-wide 30-minute default admission gate, persisted pacing across restart, shared-repository triage routing, immediate eligible work on verified bugs and notifications for feature requests/unverifiable bugs. Preserve existing pauses and deduplicate observations. GH 05–09; WORK 09–11.'],
 ['Linked GitHub issue synchronization','Create a local issue and verify creation/editing stays local. Explicitly publish or link it and verify inbound GitHub updates, same-bug clarification and new-scope decisions. Keep intermediate execution details local; only verified completion posts a resolution summary and closes remotely. Test offline/restart recovery, deduplicated uncertain comment/close outcomes, conflicting edits, authentication/rate-limit errors and visible retry state. Remote closure must preserve verified started fixes and approved feature work; retain feature requests for manual choice. Preserve independent pauses, attribution, source links and unresolved conflict evidence. GH 03–09; WORK 13–14; CONTROL 08–10.'],
 ['Standalone issue','Create a project-level issue without a feature through UI and agent tools. Find and prioritize it, then start implementation immediately without a synthetic feature or separate plan approval. Verify that unclear requirements request input while existing tool, user-pause, review and Auto-Merge policies remain effective under AGENT-15. Verify attributed usage/cost tracking without an issue spending cap, 75% budget warning or budget-triggered pause under ROUTE-12. Delegate scoped work to subagents without creating a feature. Inspect parent/child agent lineage, execution transcripts, worktree/branch/PR links, review/fix/check results, activity and artifacts on the issue; verify shared permissions, isolation and pause/redirect controls under AGENT-14. Verify the independent per-issue concurrency default of 10, a changed cap and useful parallel delegation within that cap without a global agent-count limit under AGENT-10–11. A feature link remains optional. Verify its PR targets main, apply review/fix/check and Auto-Merge policy, and exercise pause/continue and reviewer write restrictions. Verify automatic cleanup of harness-owned worktrees and local branches after confirmed merge to main, preserving transcripts, artifacts, GitHub links and complete history. Unintegrated work must survive; cleanup failure must be visible and retryable. WORK 03, 11–12; GIT 02–05, 10–12; TOOL 05.'],
 ['Priority work allocation','Create prioritized features with child tasks and standalone issues. next_work(project) and explicit feature=null select the highest-priority eligible feature or standalone issue from one project queue. Verify either kind can win by priority and feature child tasks cannot bypass their feature. next_work(project, feature) selects only that feature’s tasks. Reject wrong-project features and expose empty/blocked outcomes. Concurrent scheduled/coordinator callers and retries must not duplicate assignments or starts. Skip incomplete/unapproved features and select only features approved for implementation; preserve clear actionable issue eligibility. Honor pauses, dependencies, budgets and current approval. Verify a scheduled agent can use the same enabled tools to allocate/start work with coordinator/worker lineage and schedule provenance. WORK 09–10; JOB 07; AGENT 01–13; TOOL 01–05.'],
 ['Scheduled inference tasks','Create app/project inference schedules. Run a GitHub issue monitor through the router and permitted tools; inspect transcript, usage, actions and created work. Repeat scans/retries without duplicate effects; test scope, disabled/unavailable tools, approval, notifications, cancellation and failure. Close the app: work stops. Reopen: enabled schedules reactivate, missed occurrences stay skipped and interrupted runs resume with their history. Preserve independent holds; feature and issue agents remain paused until manual Continue. Verify an occurrence arriving during an active inference run is skipped, with no overlapping run or catch-up. JOB 01–08; ROUTE 01; TOOL 01–05; GH 04.'],
 ['Database data tools','Index schema without row-content indexing. Verify new connections default to read-only and reject mutations. Explicitly enable writes on one connection: exercise permitted inserts/updates/deletes through data tools while another connection stays read-only and disabled tools stay denied. Disable writes: direct and indirect mutations are refused while authorized reads remain available. Verify reviewer write denial even when other agents can write. Inspect connection/scope, attributed results and failures. Apply a schema change through a committed migration; when migrations are not possible, exercise a direct schema change and inspect its history. Verify both paths reject application to read-only connections or by reviewers, and a commit alone grants no execution authority. Share one connection across two projects with different access; verify enforcement and attribution, rejection of an unauthorized third project, and no write elevation over read-only connection settings. Schema indexing remains read-only. DB 01–09; TOOL 01–05; LOG 01–03.'],
 ['Task Git locations','Open tasks with individual and grouped PRs, including a task spanning repositories. Verify task-visible repository identities, current worktree paths, branches and PR links, with navigation to agent history and inspectable changes/checks/reviews. Each task covered by a grouped PR links to it. After cleanup, retain history while marking removed resources accurately. Manual staging/commit/branch/merge controls are not required; autonomous Git delivery remains available under configured policy. GIT 01–10; HISTORY 01–02.'],
 ['Fresh index and work import','Import CodeGraph projects, features, tasks and their histories; rebuild repository indexes from source. Compare selected source/target records, identities/mappings, statuses, dependencies and history/evidence links. Verify that memory, knowledge, lessons and conventions are not imported. Repeat after interruption without duplicates or silent omissions. Historical code references remain inspectable while fresh index links reconcile. Imported work does not start executing automatically. Missing source data or mapping failures are reported. IMPORT 01–02; MODEL 02; STORE 05–06.'],
 ['Repository instructions','Work in a repository with AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md plus subdirectory instructions. Verify automatic loading and the applicable directory scope, including work spanning paths/repositories. Verify that the files supplement role instructions, an explicit user direction takes precedence and a file cannot enable a disabled tool or grant reviewer writes. Retain instruction-source revisions in session context history. CONTEXT 02–03, 06; TOOL 02–05.'],
 ['Automatic context compaction','Drive an active agent to 80% of the routed model context limit and verify automatic compaction and continued work without user input. Retain the full original transcript, logical agent identity, work links and compaction history. Verify retained instructions, plan, unresolved decisions and artifact references. Exercise changed model limits, oversized tool results and compaction failure with visible bounded recovery. CONTEXT 01–05; ROUTE 01.'],
 ['Prompt cache preservation','Compare successive provider requests through text and parallel tool turns, including built-in and MCP definitions, retries and restart. Verify unchanged eligible prefixes, stable content boundaries, tool IDs and required continuation blocks. Exercise instruction/permission changes, model/upstream fallback and compaction: apply required changes and explain reduced reuse without weakening correctness. On supported endpoints inspect actual cached/uncached/write usage and total cost; unavailable metrics remain explicit. Local prefix checks must not claim a guaranteed provider hit. CACHE 01–06; ROUTE 01–10; CONTEXT 04–05.'],
 ['Project artifact sharing','Have an agent save a screenshot and report, another agent discover/read them by stable reference, and the user preview, attach and export them. Add a file through the UI and expose it through agent tools. Verify explicit originating project, feature and task links, navigation in both directions, and preservation of origin when another task reuses the artifact. Verify scope, provenance, versions and persistence after restart and worktree cleanup. Verify that age and storage pressure do not cause default deletion; retained artifacts remain until an explicit delete operation. Exercise read-only reviewer access and a storage failure. ART 01–05; GIT 06–07; STORE 05–06.'],
 ['Native input notifications','With the app unfocused, create an item requiring user input and verify a native OS notification where supported and permitted. Deny permission or suppress OS alerts and verify the in-app notification and approval item remain available. Reading/dismissing an alert does not resolve the decision or resume paused work. Validate macOS, Windows and supported Linux desktop environments. NOTIFY 01–04; REVIEW 01–03.'],
 ['Desktop takeover consent','With user activity present, request native UI control and verify the OK/Wait prompt. Until answered, and after Wait, no automation input or focus takeover occurs; independent tasks continue. After OK and resource allocation, run the authorized operation. A resource grant alone must not substitute for consent. UIQA 04–05; RES 01–03; NOTIFY 01.'],
 ['Resource reservations','Have tasks in different projects request the same desktop resource at capacity one; grant one and visibly queue the other while independent tasks continue. Inspect holder, purpose and priority; renew and release through tools. Verify retry idempotency, stale-owner rejection and recovery before reassignment after expiry. Exercise a capacity greater than one on another resource. RES 01–03; UIQA 04; AGENT 09–11.'],
 ['Agent UI testing','Use browser control to exercise a website and native control to exercise a desktop app; inspect linked actions, assertions, screenshots, logs and tested revision. Exercise missing permissions and unsupported controls. Verify browser session isolation, desktop input coordinated by resource reservations, interruption and reviewer write denial. Validate backend provisioning without preinstalled application runtimes. UIQA 01–05; RES 01–03; TOOL 05; PLAT 01–03.'],
 ['Feature plan refinement','Start incomplete planning: the coordinator plans what it can and requests approval/input before unapproved execution. A ready authorized plan proceeds without redundant approval. Refine through UI and chat before/during execution; verify visible changes, retained history, reconciliation and rejection of stale results. Task breakdown, delegation, sequencing and PR grouping may change autonomously within approved requirements and policies. Propose changed scope or acceptance criteria: require explicit user approval before executing that change while valid unblocked work continues. Verify both approval and rejection outcomes, and correctness evidence. AGENT 12–13; WORK 08; CONTROL 01–04.'],
 ['Selective resume','Close the main window with multiple features and issues active; verify pause/save/exit. Reopen into workspace chat with their agents paused. Resume selected features and issues together and verify that unselected work and unresolved blocked tasks do not start. CONTROL 08–10; REVIEW 03.'],
 ['Sleep wake and keep awake','With the app open and feature and issue agents, maintenance and inference jobs running, sleep and wake the computer; eligible work continues automatically, while existing user pauses and blockers remain. Close and reopen to verify that features and issues instead require manual selection. Verify keep-awake defaults to off on all three platforms. Enable it for an idle open app; verify the display may turn off while work continues, and release on disable/exit. CONTROL 08–11; POWER 01–02; JOB 03–04.'],
 ['Concurrent feature work','With independent ready tasks and capacity available, run multiple tasks simultaneously in distinct task worktrees and refill completed slots up to the per-feature cap. Verify coordinator integration also uses a worktree, the primary checkout is unchanged, and task/worktree history remains linked. Verify the default of 10, a changed cap, independent allowances for simultaneously active features without a global agent-count cap, and continued progress while another task waits for user input. AGENT 09–11; GIT 02; REVIEW 03.'],
 ['Feature branch delivery','Run several tasks for a multi-repository feature in isolated worktrees. Have the coordinator group related tasks into one PR and use another PR for separate work; all target their repository feature branches. Verify no PR to main is opened before feature implementation completion. Then open final feature PRs to main, retain task/group PR lineage, and enforce review/fix/check and Auto-Merge policies. Keep required resources during pending delivery and perform cleanup after verified integration. GIT 02, 06–09; DELIVERY 01–08.'],
 ['Feature Git cleanup','After verified final delivery of a multi-repository feature, verify automatic removal of all its harness-created task/integration worktrees and local branches. Confirm that commits, transcripts, PR/review links and historical ownership remain browsable, and unrelated user resources remain intact. Exercise a cleanup failure and verify visible status and retry without data loss. GIT 02, 06–07, 09; HISTORY 01–02.'],
 ['Provider connections and routing','Configure Codex sign-in, optional direct Claude subscription sign-in with explicit risk acceptance, a separate Anthropic API-key connection, OpenRouter credentials, and local/remote OpenAI-compatible endpoints, including Ollama and LM Studio. Verify separate credentials, route eligibility and billing; no implicit subscription/API switch. Exercise supported discovery, connection testing, refresh, reauthentication and disconnect. Verify that chat, workers, reviews, dreaming, embeddings, context summaries/titles and accepted MCP inference use the internal router with attributed usage and visible failures. ROUTE 01–11; MCP 05.'],
 ['Dependency installation','Have an implementation agent install a project dependency within authorized scope without an approval prompt. Request a system-wide tool/package installation; verify execution waits for user approval and denial prevents installation. Verify disabled tools and reviewer read-only restrictions still apply, with attributable installation and approval history. TOOL 05–07; CONTROL 01–07.'],
 ['Harness code self-improvement','Have the harness propose an improvement to its Rust code, plan it as project work, and execute the approved scope in isolated worktrees through implementation, checks, PR review/fixes and configured delivery. Verify permissions, plan approval, required reviews and merge policy remain effective. Verify documentation is included in the plan and delivered changes; an implementation with missing or stale required Help/developer documentation must fail completion and delivery review. Inspect linked transcripts, Git/review history, documentation changes, build artifacts and evidence. Distinguish merged/tested source from the running app. Pending or denied installation/restart approval leaves the installed version running; PR merge alone must not activate a build. Approve an update: verify recorded approval/outcomes, safe shutdown and selective manual feature/issue resume. SELF 01–04; HELP 02–06; AGENT 12–13; GIT 02, 08–10; DELIVERY 01–08; CONTROL 08–10.'],
 ['Branch and worktree overlays','Create two worktrees with different committed and uncommitted changes, including staged edits, an indexable untracked file, deletion and rename. Verify each agent automatically queries its own view across search, source, traversal, impact and context tools; main and the other worktree remain unchanged. Check explicit cross-view inspection, revision/local-snapshot provenance, confidence and unresolved relationships. Move the baseline and edit during indexing: reject stale or mixed snapshots and report rebuild progress. Exercise request reuse, missing/failed/expired views and retries. Cleanup retires derived views without deleting history or artifacts. Baseline-only analysis and unavailable semantic coverage must remain explicit. CODE 05–11; GIT 02, 06–07; LOG 01–03.'],
 ['Chat model selector and session pinning','In every composer, choose a router model and verify it persists until changed. Select Auto; verify the pin survives turns, tools, compaction and app restarts. Rate-limit or disable the pinned model: automatically switch to an eligible available replacement and retain its pin after restart and original-model recovery. Restoring pins must not resume paused features/issues. Sessions remain independent, routed and attributed. Verify capability/permission constraints and attachment retention. With all eligible models cooling, verify no early retries, automatic reconnection at the earliest expiry and updated cooldown handling; unrelated holds remain. Missing retry times and capability changes await review. UI 02, 10; ROUTE 01–05, 13–15.'],
 ['Chat attachments and vision','In every chat composer, attach images and files using the selection dialog, drag-and-drop and paste. Verify correct message/session association, retained files and applicable work/artifact links after switching chats, restart and compaction. Ask an agent to inspect a screenshot/image artifact; verify a vision-capable model/endpoint is selected through the router and visual content reaches it. Exercise unavailable vision routes and unsupported/failed file processing with visible outcomes and no false analysis claim. UI 02–04, 08–09; ROUTE 01, 13; ART 01–05; CONTEXT 04–05.'],
 ['Built-in web research','Verify web search and page fetching work as built-in tools without an external MCP server and are enabled by default. Exercise coordinator, implementation and read-only review access, source URLs, recorded results and work attribution. Disable each tool in Settings and verify invocation is denied. Backend selection, configuration and result-handling details follow the selected implementation contract. TOOL 01–05, 08; HISTORY 01–02.'],
 ['External tool control','Verify implementation agents have shell/process tools enabled by default; disable them and verify denial at invocation. Connect to an external MCP server as a client; verify read-only tools start enabled. Connect an independent harness to the DevWorks MCP server and retrieve indexed code, planned work and Knowledge with citations and scope isolation; verify default write denial, explicit work/Knowledge grants, revocation, receipt recovery and app-close unavailability across stdio and HTTP. Change individual toggles and use Select all; verify the effective agent tool set matches the saved selection and invocations retain attribution. Verify the shared selection across coordinator, coding and review agents, including the same selected read tools. Verify that reviewer mutations through built-in, MCP and shell/process tools are refused by the harness even after Select all; findings are retained and fixes go to an authorized implementation agent. Disabled tools are refused at invocation, saved choices survive discovery, and an unavailable server affects only dependent work. MCP 01–11; TOOL 01–06; SET 01–02.'],
 ['Autonomous review and delivery','Verify Auto-Merge and mandatory independent review both start enabled. Test project and repository overrides independently for each setting, with repository overrides taking precedence and effective values and sources visible. With mandatory review enabled, verify configured GitHub automated reviews satisfy the gate without a duplicate reviewer while automation is working; failed or stalled automation uses an independent in-app fallback. For a repository without automated reviews configured, verify a separate in-app reviewer and fix cycle. In both cases, required reviews must complete and all actionable findings must be resolved before merge. Auto-Merge off stops before merge; Auto-Merge on still cannot merge with any unresolved actionable finding. Verify the default limit of 3 fix cycles, a changed limit, and escalation plus notification when the coordinator cannot reach a decision. Unblocked feature tasks continue while the affected task waits; feature-wide waiting requires a dependency that blocks the remaining work. DELIVERY 01–08; REVIEW 03.'],
 ['Deployment and release authority','Configure deployment and release-publishing policies independently at workspace, project and repository levels. Verify both default to Ask me, inherited values and override sources are visible, and Auto-Merge does not authorize either action. Test approval and rejection for Ask me, refusal for Disabled, and permitted automatic actions under existing tool permissions. Different repositories and the two action types retain independent policies. A self-improvement build still requires installation/restart approval. DELIVERY 01, 04, 09; SELF 01–03; TOOL 01–05; REVIEW 01–03.'],
 ['GitHub and settings','Connect one GitHub.com account; discover personal and organization repositories. Associate repositories from different owners with a project and inspect authorization and PR/check/review state. Verify authorized organization delivery and visible permission denial. Across chat, tasks, histories and notifications, every referenced GitHub resource is a clickable link to the correct owner/repository resource. Edit active role instructions: persist and adopt at the next safe step without restart. Have an agent propose a revision: pending/rejected changes must not affect active instructions; approved changes adopt at the next safe step. Inspect history and rollback, retaining reviewer write denial. Navigate Settings without losing chat. GH 01–04; SET 01–06; TOOL 05.'],
 ['Database and graph exploration','For SQLite, MariaDB, MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQL Server, configure a source, index its supported schema, navigate objects and relationships, inspect freshness, and remove indexed metadata without changing the source. Verify each engine independently, including read tools and permitted writes versus denied writes. Report adapter/version coverage. Reject unsupported HelixDB source configuration. Use distinctive source values and verify that only schema metadata persists, with no records/property values in indexing results, logs, transcripts, artifacts, caches or embeddings, including failure paths. From a repository home, traverse Overview/Nodes/Health/Reviews, internal code-project and node-type drill-down, container/member lists, node details, source and incoming/outgoing cross-repository links; verify repository breadcrumbs and return context. DB 01–07; SCHEMA 01–02; GRAPH 01–04.'],
 ['Operational visibility','Run a manual operation and a scheduled job. Inspect logs for partial or failed indexing, retry safely, and distinguish repository health findings from app service health. Review code findings against the correct revision. After reopening, verify enabled schedules use future due times with no catch-up for missed occurrences; interrupted maintenance/inference runs resume automatically while feature and issue agents remain paused. JOB 01–06; OP 01–02; LOG 01–03; HEALTH 01–02; CODEREVIEW 01–02.'],
 ['Conventions and skills','Discover an existing shared skill from ~/.agents/skills and create a harness-specific skill in the dedicated directory. Verify that both are manageable and show their origin/path. For same-name skills, verify that the harness-specific version is active and the shared file remains intact. Select scope and enabled state, then verify that a relevant agent receives the correct versions under its effective tool policy. Edit a skill during active work and verify adoption at the next safe step, with the version transition in session history. Load a skill’s referenced document and asset, then run an authorized helper script. Verify disabled-tool enforcement, project scope, reviewer read-only restrictions and system-install approval; missing optional runtimes must be visible without preventing core app use. Have an agent propose a new harness-specific skill and an edit: neither may alter active skills or become available before approval. Reject one, approve another; inspect agent/work attribution, rationale, reviewed revisions, outcomes and safe-step adoption. Propose an override of a shared skill: pending/rejected versions leave selection unchanged; approval activates the harness override at the next safe step. Verify shared originals stay untouched and source/override provenance is retained. Exercise proposed stale-approval reconciliation and rollback; distinguish validation from claimed effectiveness. Also create or import a convention. CONV 01–02; SKILL 01–09; TOOL 02–07; PLAT 03.']])
